                    The Original Article  was published on 22 May 2024

arising from W. Zhou et al. Nature Geoscience https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-024-01441-1 (2025)The Arctic has been warming nearly four times faster than the rest of the planet over past decades. Zhou et al.1 (hereafter ZLL24) argue that only threefold Arctic amplification is due to external forcing, and attribute the remaining amplification to internal variability, in particular an Arctic mode of surface temperature changes. Here we attribute this apparent Arctic mode to a statistical artefact that arises when linearly detrending a nonlinear time series prior to empirical orthogonal function (EOF) analysis. Our results suggest that the analysis in ZLL24 cannot reliably quantify the contribution of internal variability to observed Arctic amplification.

